WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.366
This is not the bundled sample.

00:00:02.366 --> 00:00:09.236
It is a recording from a CANedge logger, in native MDF four,
published by CSS Electronics.

00:00:09.236 --> 00:00:14.579
Nine thousand six hundred frames across fifty arbitration
identifiers,

00:00:14.579 --> 00:00:17.174
every one of them twenty-nine bit.

00:00:17.174 --> 00:00:21.831
That usually means a truck on J1939, but these are NMEA 2000:

00:00:21.831 --> 00:00:23.815
this logger was on a boat.

00:00:23.815 --> 00:00:30.456
Until now the MDF four reader had only ever been given files
this project wrote itself.

00:00:30.456 --> 00:00:35.910
The sniffer collapses the bus to one row per message instead
of one row per frame.

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:41.630
A byte turns green when it rises and red when it falls, and
fades back after a second.

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:47.616
Bytes that have never moved stay dim, so what is alive on this
bus is visible at a glance.

00:00:47.616 --> 00:00:50.013
Notch is the reason to use this view.

00:00:50.013 --> 00:00:55.001
It records every bit that is currently in motion and ignores
it from then on.

00:00:55.001 --> 00:01:01.156
On this log that masks four hundred and sixty four bits of
counters and checksums in one press,

00:01:01.156 --> 00:01:02.905
and the display goes quiet.

00:01:02.905 --> 00:01:07.440
From that point the only thing that lights up is something you
caused.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:12.807
The offline detectors run over the real log with no prior
knowledge of the vehicle:

00:01:12.807 --> 00:01:18.562
rolling counters, checksum bytes and the algorithm that
reproduces them, bit level flags,

00:01:18.562 --> 00:01:23.606
enumerated bytes, field boundaries from bit entropy, and
multiplexed messages.

00:01:23.606 --> 00:01:28.714
On this capture that is twenty four counters, six flags,
fourteen enumerations,

00:01:28.714 --> 00:01:32.400
forty two field boundaries and four multiplexed messages.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.755
The same sweep goes below byte level:

00:01:34.755 --> 00:01:37.555
single bit switches and small packed fields,

00:01:37.555 --> 00:01:41.565
and bytes that behave as enumerations rather than
measurements.

00:01:41.565 --> 00:01:45.830
On this marine log that is six flags and fourteen enumerated
bytes,

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:48.312
each with the states actually observed.

00:01:48.312 --> 00:01:52.190
Entropy per bit finds where one field ends and the next
begins,

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:55.022
without being told anything about the vehicle.

00:01:55.022 --> 00:01:58.285
Forty two candidate field boundaries on this capture,

00:01:58.285 --> 00:02:03.456
each with a confidence that is a match fraction over the
frames loaded, not a proof.

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:09.710
The signals view gives one row per message with its rate,
payload entropy and a suspected type.

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:14.582
NMEA 2000 identifiers carry a parameter group number and a
source address,

00:02:14.582 --> 00:02:20.112
so the shape of the traffic is already meaningful before a
single signal is defined.

00:02:20.112 --> 00:02:25.419
Wind speed, defined on the wind message, decodes real frames
through cantools,

00:02:25.419 --> 00:02:29.161
with the bit grid showing exactly which bits it claims.

00:02:29.161 --> 00:02:32.019
Because the identifier is twenty-nine bit,

00:02:32.019 --> 00:02:36.849
the exported file has to set the extended flag in its frame
identifier.

00:02:36.849 --> 00:02:40.319
Exporting this capture is what found a genuine bug:

00:02:40.319 --> 00:02:45.354
the openpilot writer was emitting the bare number, which
cantools refuses.

00:02:45.354 --> 00:02:49.368
Every writer is now checked against an extended identifier.

00:02:49.368 --> 00:02:54.935
Plotted against time, the decoded signal is what confirms or
refutes the definition.

00:02:54.935 --> 00:03:01.099
This one stays between nought point seven two and nought point
eight seven metres per second,

00:03:01.099 --> 00:03:04.148
light air, and moves like a physical quantity.

00:03:04.148 --> 00:03:07.197
A sawtooth would mean the byte order is wrong.

00:03:07.197 --> 00:03:10.776
This is the step that turns a candidate into a signal.

00:03:10.776 --> 00:03:16.852
The same real log is written out as SavvyCAN C S V, Vector B L
F, Vector A S C,

00:03:16.852 --> 00:03:21.621
candump and socket C A N pcap, then read back by every parser.

00:03:21.621 --> 00:03:28.851
All five have to return the same nine thousand six hundred
frames, the same fifty identifiers,

00:03:28.851 --> 00:03:32.697
the same payload bytes and the same extended flag.

00:03:32.697 --> 00:03:33.312
They do.

00:03:33.312 --> 00:03:34.864
A second real recording:

00:03:34.864 --> 00:03:40.750
twenty-three minutes from a two channel logger, one hundred
and fifty five thousand frames.

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:44.178
Both channels keep their bus tags through the reader,

00:03:44.178 --> 00:03:47.089
which the single bus sample could never test.

00:03:47.089 --> 00:03:51.552
The sniffer folds the whole thing into sixteen rows in half a
second.

00:03:51.552 --> 00:03:53.609
The largest of the real logs:

00:03:53.609 --> 00:03:59.427
one hundred and forty five thousand frames, one hundred and
forty two identifiers,

00:03:59.427 --> 00:04:03.329
and this one really is J1939, extended from end to end.

00:04:03.329 --> 00:04:09.784
It loads, it parses, and every arbitration identifier above
seven hundred and ninety one is

00:04:09.784 --> 00:04:14.112
correctly reported as extended rather than quietly truncated.

00:04:14.112 --> 00:04:17.622
Through all of this the transmit gate stayed shut.

00:04:17.622 --> 00:04:24.222
No bus was opened, nothing was sent, and none of the twenty
five tools the assistant interface

00:04:24.222 --> 00:04:26.679
exposes is able to transmit at all.

00:04:26.679 --> 00:04:31.383
Ninety checks over real data and five hundred and forty unit
tests.

00:04:31.383 --> 00:04:33.629
Three real bugs found and fixed:

00:04:33.629 --> 00:04:36.788
the openpilot writer on extended identifiers,

00:04:36.788 --> 00:04:40.720
the sniffer ageing a loaded log against wall clock time,

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:44.160
and the decoder reading this marine bus as J1939.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:47.770
Synthetic data agrees with whatever the code assumes.

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:53.832
Real data from somebody else's hardware does not, which is the
whole point of running it.
